    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u give me a quick breakdown of the stages in a standard CI/CD pipeline? specifically looking for code commit, testing, staging, and prod deployment. also need to know what kind of gates we should have at each step to make sure nothing breaks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! Here’s a quick breakdown of the stages in a CI/CD pipeline with recommended gates:\n\n1. **Code Commit**: Developers push code to a repository. *Gate*: Automatic build trigger upon new commit – ensure the code matches basic format and passes initial lint checks.\n\n2. **Continuous Integration (CI)**: The code is built, unit tests are run, and code quality checks are performed. *Gate*: Failed unit tests, failing code quality checks, or compilation errors prevent the build from proceeding.\n\n3. **Continuous Deployment (CD) - Staging Environment**: The application is deployed to a staging environment for further testing. *Gate*:  End-to-end testing fails, CI tests against staging fail, or significant configuration issues prevented propagation to this environment.\n\n4. **Production (Prod) Deployment**: The application is deployed to production. *Gate*: Manual approval, pre-deployment checks, and possibly a final round of tests to ensure stability and no regressions. Once live, monitoring and rollback mechanisms are in place. \n\nThese gates help ensure that only stable, well-reviewed code reaches production.",
      "source": "target",
